Causal chain (why resistance spreads even as hospital A cuts use):
1. Selection — cutting use weakens local enrichment but doesn't instantly reverse it: sub-inhibitory drug concentrations, pharmacokinetic variation, and co-selection (multi-resistance plasmids; use of drug B keeps gene A) maintain a residual advantage; resistant subpopulations established before the cut don't vanish overnight.
2. HGT — resistance genes sit on conjugative plasmids/transposons/integrons that move between strains and species within patients, within wards, and across wards; gene frequency can rise even when the original host clone is suppressed, and one plasmid can carry multiple resistance determinants, so selection by any one drug selects for all.
3. Fitness cost heterogeneity — costs of resistance vary by genetic background and plasmid; compensatory mutations and low-cost plasmid variants make some lineages nearly cost-free, so they don't recede when the drug is removed. Because cost is heterogeneous, the subset of lineages that happen to be cheap persists and keeps exporting resistance genes.
4. Spatial metapopulation — wards/hospitals are nodes coupled by patient transfers. Other hospitals keep high antibiotic pressure, so resistance amplifies there; colonized patients continually import resistance into the focal hospital. Local prevalence = local growth + import − export, so even with negative local growth, import alone can drive prevalence upward.

